### What is the problem?

Given the root of a binary tree, return its node values level by level but from the bottom level up to the root, left to right within each level. For example, a tree with levels `[1]`, `[2,3]`, `[4,5,6]` returns `[[4,5,6], [2,3], [1]]`.

At the National Bank of India, the audit report must show the hierarchy from clerks up to the branch manager.

### How does standard BFS level-order work?

Use a FIFO queue. At each level, record the queue size, process exactly that many nodes, enqueue their children, and collect the level's values.

```java
// Standard top-down level order
Queue<TreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.offer(root);
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int levelSize = queue.size();
    List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
        TreeNode node = queue.poll();
        level.add(node.val);
        if (node.left != null) queue.offer(node.left);
        if (node.right != null) queue.offer(node.right);
    }
    result.add(level); // top-down: append
}
```

### How do you make it bottom-up?

Change one line: instead of appending each level, prepend it.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.*;

public class ReverseLevelOrder {
    public static List<List<Integer>> reverseLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {
        LinkedList<List<Integer>> result = new LinkedList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.offer(root);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int levelSize = queue.size();
            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
                TreeNode node = queue.poll();
                level.add(node.val);
                if (node.left != null) queue.offer(node.left);
                if (node.right != null) queue.offer(node.right);
            }
            result.addFirst(level); // bottom-up: prepend
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Tree:     1
        //          / \
        //         2   3
        //        / \
        //       4   5
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1,
            new TreeNode(2, new TreeNode(4), new TreeNode(5)),
            new TreeNode(3));
        System.out.println(reverseLevelOrder(root));
    }
}
```

```text
[[4, 5], [2, 3], [1]]
```

### Why use LinkedList instead of ArrayList for prepending?

`LinkedList.addFirst()` is O(1) because it adjusts head pointers. `ArrayList.add(0, x)` is O(n) because it shifts all existing elements one position to the right. Over all levels, this turns a linear algorithm into a quadratic one.

```java
// Wrong: O(n) per insert -> O(n^2) total
ArrayList<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
result.add(0, level); // shifts all existing levels

// Correct: O(1) per insert -> O(n) total
LinkedList<List<Integer>> result = new LinkedList<>();
result.addFirst(level); // no shifting
```

### What is the alternative approach?

Collect levels top-down into an ArrayList, then reverse the list once at the end.

```java
List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
// ... standard BFS adding levels to result ...
Collections.reverse(result); // O(number of levels)
```

Both approaches are O(n) overall. The prepend approach avoids the explicit reverse call.

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Aspect | Complexity |
|---|---|
| Time | O(n), visit each node once |
| Space | O(n) for the queue + O(n) for the result |

### What are common mistakes?

Using `ArrayList.add(0, level)` instead of `LinkedList.addFirst(level)`, which silently degrades to O(n^2).

Forgetting the null check for the root, which causes a NullPointerException when the tree is empty.

### What are the edge cases?

- **Empty tree (null root)**: return an empty list.
- **Single node**: return `[[value]]`.
- **Skewed tree (all left children)**: each level has one node; result is reversed single-element levels.

### What should a senior engineer know?

This problem tests whether you understand that the traversal order and the accumulation order are independent. BFS always processes top-down, but how you store the results determines the output order.

The same pattern applies to zigzag level order, where even levels go left-to-right and odd levels go right-to-left. The BFS is the same; only the per-level collection order changes.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Zigzag level order? (Alternate the direction within each level using a deque or reverse.)
- Average of levels? (Compute sum and count per level.)
- Right-side view of the tree? (Take the last node of each level.)
